#!/bin/sh
# Clear stale "working" dots by asking the agent itself, not by trusting our
# event history. Claude Code sets the pane's OSC title to a spinner glyph while
# it works and to "✳ <summary>" the moment it stops:
#
#   ◐ Tmux agent counter subagents display    working
#   ✳ Resend to TEM migration verification    stopped — at the prompt
#
# So a window whose @agent_state says "running" while every Claude pane in it
# shows ✳ is lying, and this puts it back to done ● — not idle ○, because
# reaching this line means nothing told us the turn ended, so you certainly
# did not watch it end. agent-seen opens the dot once the tab has been in
# front of you, and runs from here too as its own backstop.
#
# Why this exists at all: every other input to the dot is an EVENT, and an event
# that never arrives leaves the last one standing. Claude Code fires no hook on
# an ESC interrupt (anthropics/claude-code#9516, which agent-interrupt-state
# covers from the other end), and a turn that dies on a terminal API error —
# context blown past what compaction can save, a tool call that can't be parsed
# after a retry — ends via StopFailure instead of Stop. Miss one and the tab sits
# blue forever, which is the worst way to be wrong: blue reads as "leave this one
# alone", so a finished agent goes unnoticed for as long as you believe the dot.
# The title is a different kind of signal. It says what is true now rather than
# what happened once, so nothing can latch: whatever sequence of events got the
# option wrong, the next sweep sets it right.
#
# It demotes running → done, and promotes done/idle → running. Both directions
# come from the same premise — the title says what is true NOW — so trusting it
# one way and not the other is what let a single bad reading latch for a whole
# turn. Three deliberate limits:
#
#   - needs-input is never touched, in either direction. A tab blocked on a
#     permission prompt also shows ✳ (Claude isn't working, it's waiting on you),
#     so demoting red would turn "answer me" into "nothing to see here" — the one
#     direction worse than a stale dot.
#   - @agent_bg is honoured. agent-stop-state sets it when a turn ended with
#     agent-driven work still attached that will re-wake the session on its own;
#     that blue is deliberate, and Claude is legitimately at the prompt meanwhile.
#   - Windows with no Claude pane are invisible here. Codex and Antigravity tabs
#     never write these titles, so nothing matches and their dots are left to
#     their own hooks.
#
# ── the ✳ that does not mean "stopped" ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The demotion used to run on the bare ✳ test, with no debounce, on the strength
# of a working tab sampled every 250ms for 22s never once dropping its spinner.
# That measurement was taken mid-turn, and both ways this goes wrong live at the
# edges of a turn instead:
#
#   1. A session that has not written a title yet. Claude only starts driving
#      the title once the conversation has a summary; before that the pane holds
#      the boot title, "✳ Claude Code" — a ✳ that means "no summary yet", not
#      "at the prompt". Measured on a fresh session: the turn began at t=2.5s
#      and the sweep demoted it at t=4.5s, one status tick in, with the model
#      still working 35s later. That is EVERY new session's first turn, which is
#      why this was worth chasing rather than tolerating.
#   2. The title lags the hook at the start of a turn. UserPromptSubmit sets
#      running the instant you press enter; the first spinner frame arrives some
#      time after. A tick landing in that gap reads the previous turn's ✳.
#
# Three guards, each aimed at one part of that:
#
#   - @agent_title_spins — a window is only demotable once a spinner has ACTUALLY
#     been seen in it, which is this window's own proof that its Claude drives
#     the title. Until then the ✳ is not evidence of anything and the window is
#     left alone. (Same shape as @agent_focus_reporting in agent-seen: remember
#     whether the signal has ever been observed before reading meaning into its
#     absence.) agent-state clears it with the rest, so a new session in a reused
#     window starts unproven again.
#   - @agent_sweep_stopped — the debounce the old comment prescribed: the ✳ has
#     to survive two consecutive sweeps before it counts. Costs 5s on a genuine
#     stale-blue fix, which is a backstop nobody is watching the clock on.
#   - the promotion — whatever still slips through heals on the next tick, since
#     a spinner over a done ● dot is the same kind of lie in the other direction.
#     It also covers a running that was never set at all, if a turn-start hook
#     ever fails to fire.
#
# Run from tmux-agent-count, on the status-interval tick — see the note there on
# why the counter is the right place to hang a reconciler.
#
# No-op outside tmux. Always exits 0.

command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0

# The glyph list below is borrowed data, not something we derived, and it is the
# one part of this script that goes stale — Claude has already changed it once,
# from braille to half-circles at 2.1.228. Upstream is herdr, which tracks these
# for a living:
#
#   source:  https://herdr.dev/agent-detection/claude.toml
#            (rules "osc_title_working" and "osc_title_idle"; same bytes as
#            website/agent-detection/claude.toml in github.com/herdrdev/herdr,
#            read at v0.8.1 / 5203a5dc0f39a082938ea0f9836d6257ea7e155f, Apache-2.0)
#   check:   curl -s https://herdr.dev/agent-detection/claude.toml | awk '/osc_title/,/^$/'
#
# Upstream matches ^[\x{2800}-\x{28FF}\x{25D0}-\x{25D3}] for working and ^\x{2733}
# for idle. We deliberately implement a subset: the quarter-circles only, and no
# trailing-space requirement. Dropping braille costs only the "some pane is
# working" veto — a title can't start with a spinner and ✳ at once, so the worst
# case is a window with two Claude panes where one is on a pre-2.1.228 build. The
# ✳ test is unchanged across versions. What a refresh is looking for is a
# codepoint upstream has ADDED that we don't match; the symptom is a busy tab
# going yellow while it is plainly still working. Check a live pane before
# believing that symptom, though — it has a second cause with nothing to do with
# upstream, and that one turned out to be the real one the first time it showed
# up (see "the ✳ that does not mean stopped" above). Glyphs verified against a
# live 2.1.237 pane. See AGENTS.md, "Borrowed from herdr", for the rest of what
# came from there.
now=$(date +%s)
tmux list-panes -a -F '#{window_id}	#{@agent_state}	#{@agent_bg}	#{@agent_title_spins}	#{@agent_sweep_stopped}	#{pane_title}' 2>/dev/null \
    | awk -F'\t' -v idle='✳' -v s1='◐' -v s2='◑' -v s3='◒' -v s4='◓' '
        {
            win = $1; state[win] = $2; bg[win] = $3
            spins[win] = $4; armed[win] = $5; t = $6
            # index(…) == 1 is "starts with" whether this awk counts bytes or
            # characters, which keeps the test independent of the locale.
            if (index(t, idle) == 1) stopped[win] = 1
            else if (index(t, s1) == 1 || index(t, s2) == 1 ||
                     index(t, s3) == 1 || index(t, s4) == 1) working[win] = 1
        }
        END {
            for (w in state) {
                # First spinner ever seen here: this window has now proved its
                # Claude drives the title, which is what makes a later ✳ mean
                # something. One write, once per session per window.
                if (working[w] && spins[w] == "") print "spins	" w

                # A blue dot the title contradicts — but only where the title is
                # known to be driven, and only on the second sweep in a row.
                if (state[w] == "running" && bg[w] == "" && spins[w] != "" \
                    && stopped[w] && !working[w])
                    print (armed[w] == "" ? "arm	" : "demote	") w
                else if (armed[w] != "")
                    print "disarm	" w

                # A yellow dot the title contradicts. No @agent_title_spins gate:
                # the spinner IS the evidence here, so there is nothing to prove
                # first. !stopped keeps a two-Claude-pane window honest — one
                # finished and one working stays on the finished dot, matching
                # the done-outranks-running ladder.
                if ((state[w] == "done" || state[w] == "idle") \
                    && working[w] && !stopped[w])
                    print "promote	" w
            }
        }
    ' 2>/dev/null \
    | while IFS='	' read -r verb win; do
        [ -n "$win" ] || continue
        case "$verb" in
            spins)
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" '@agent_title_spins' 1 2>/dev/null || true
                ;;
            arm)
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" '@agent_sweep_stopped' 1 2>/dev/null || true
                ;;
            disarm)
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" -u '@agent_sweep_stopped' 2>/dev/null || true
                ;;
            demote)
                # Written straight rather than through agent-state, which only
                # ever acts on its own $TMUX_PANE and so can't reach another
                # window. @agent_since is restamped because this IS a state
                # change, and it feeds the overview's "your turn · 4m11s".
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" '@agent_since' "$now" 2>/dev/null || true
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" '@agent_state' done 2>/dev/null || true
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" -u '@agent_sweep_stopped' 2>/dev/null || true
                # The subagent count goes too. It is stale here by construction:
                # reaching this line means @agent_bg was unset, so the last Stop
                # found no agent-driven work attached, and @agent_subagents can
                # only be non-zero when there was some. The two disagree, and the
                # dot was the one that turned out to be wrong. Left alone, a turn
                # that died without a Stop would keep adding its dead fan-out to
                # the bar's "6+11" until the session ended.
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" -u '@agent_subagents' 2>/dev/null || true
                ;;
            promote)
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" '@agent_since' "$now" 2>/dev/null || true
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" '@agent_state' running 2>/dev/null || true
                # @agent_bg would be stale on anything we promote (agent-state
                # drops it on every transition, so a done dot cannot carry one),
                # and left behind it would veto the next honest demotion.
                tmux set-option -wt "$win" -u '@agent_bg' 2>/dev/null || true
                ;;
        esac
    done

# Whatever the sweep just demoted, plus anything an earlier turn left on done,
# is resolved against what is actually on your screen. Runs unconditionally: the
# tmux hooks in dot_tmux.conf catch a window becoming visible, and this catches
# the rest — a turn that finished while you were already sitting in the tab, or
# a hook that never fired.
"$(dirname "$0")/agent-seen"

exit 0
